A seat checks its mail: petitions arrive as a step in the round

Petitions could be filed but nothing made anyone read them. A seat now checks what has been filed
for its repository as part of its session, and **doing that is the sign of life** — the thing that
distinguishes a seat that is held from a schedule that fires.

## The load-bearing change is the quiet rule

`METHOD.md` says an empty range ends the session, and it should — an advocate that manufactures
opinion because a schedule fired is the failure the whole design guards against.

But **petitions arrive on nobody's commit schedule.** A repository can go a month without a merge
and still be owed a reading. Left alone, the empty-range short circuit fires first and the one thing
a seat is asked to come looking for is the one thing it never reaches.

So: **an unread petition is part of the range.** A quiet subject with new mail is a non-quiet
session. Everything else here is plumbing for that sentence.

## Scope is not widened — the mail is delivered

`METHOD.md` forbids reading a repository that is not in the checkout, and that refusal is most of
what keeps a seat narrow. It is untouched.

`bin/petitions.mjs` runs in the **mechanical half**, before the agent wakes, and writes
`PETITIONS.md` into the workspace the seat already reads. The seat does not go anywhere to get its
post. Following an item back to where it was filed is still forbidden, and is now named in the
refusal list explicitly.

## What a seat may do with an item

**A petition addressed to the repository is not automatically the seat's.** For each unread item,
exactly one of: take it into `COMPLAINTS.md`/`ASKS.md` as a draft; say it belongs to another seat;
or flag it as nobody's. All three are complete answers and all three go in the session note.

Declining is free. **Silence is not** — an unread petition that goes unmentioned is the failure
the step exists to prevent.

An item no seat can take gets reported as outside every seat. That is a signal the repository may be
missing one, and seating stays a person's act — `METHOD.md`'s "you never propose a new seat" is not
suspended by this, and the new step says so.

## Addressing

An item is addressed by **library path** (`FCCN-ANTIBODY/civic-node`), resolved from a node's
`library/PETITIONS.yml`. A repository never declares its own address — it is discovered by matching
origin against what the library already points at, so there is nothing per-repo to configure and
nothing to keep true in two places. That was the "feels a little heavy" concern: no petitioner seat
anywhere, no config in any subject repository.

**Addressing never requires hydration.** Aliases resolve from `.gitmodules`, not by reading the
mount — otherwise a repository would go unaddressable exactly when the node had not fetched it,
which is backwards. There is a test for it against a deliberately absent mount.

**No petition space is the normal case.** Every path returns empty rather than failing: no node, a
node with no config, a malformed config, or a repository the library has no name for. The framework
is the thing that travels; the node is one operator's arrangement.

## Verified

`npm test` green, including `test/petitions.test.mjs` (new, wired into the suite). The test that
matters walks the real sequence: seat → quiet second run → **new petition with no commits opens a
work order**. Also covered: ssh/https URL parity, alias resolution without hydration, unaddressable
repos, absent and malformed configs, and content-digest read tracking (an edited petition is news
again; a merely touched one is not).

End-to-end against a scratch repo through the real `run.sh` — the order reads
*"nothing merged — this order is open for the mail alone."*

## Two fixes found on the way

- **`norm()` stripped `.git` before the trailing slash**, so `…/repo.git/` kept a `.git` nobody
  else would have. The runner's sed in `station-node` has the same ordering; parity between them is
  the invariant (a repository that can be served must be reachable), so that one is fixed on the
  matching `station-node` PR.
- **The `import.meta.url === file://${argv[1]}` main-module check fails behind a symlink.** On macOS
  `/tmp` is `/private/tmp`, so the CLI silently did nothing. Compared through `realpath` now.

## Deliberately not done

- **No seat has read anything yet.** This makes the mail arrive; the backlog is still all `draft`.
- **`writes:` is untouched.** Adopting a petition is a seat writing on its own branch, which is what
  it could already do. No new grant, no pull request from a petition.
- **Nothing writes back to the space.** Clearing an item is a person's act at triage. A seat that
  could delete its own mail is a shape worth refusing.

## Depends on

`tiliv/station-node#1` for the space itself. This is safe to merge first and does nothing until a
node exists — which is the intended resting state for any repository without one.
